<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">
  <PropertyGroup>
    <OutputType>Exe</OutputType>
    <TargetFramework>net8.0-windows</TargetFramework>
    <UseWindowsForms>true</UseWindowsForms>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
  </PropertyGroup>
  <ItemGroup>
    <!--
      The logger is a real dependency rather than a hand-rolled console writer because the scanner now
      writes levels, and the level it writes at is configuration: a bulk repair over a hundred thousand
      rows is unreadable at the detail a single asset is best debugged at. Microsoft.Extensions.Logging is
      the same abstraction every service in this stack uses, so its output is shaped the same way and the
      deployer's log store reads it without a special case.
    -->
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.Logging" Version="8.0.1" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.Logging.Console" Version="8.0.1" />
    <!--
      The catalogue is a SQLite database, and this is the provider that reads and writes it. It is the
      same package at the same version the indexing service and the API already carry, so a deployment
      ends up with one native library rather than three that could drift apart.
    -->
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Data.Sqlite" Version="8.0.10" />
  </ItemGroup>
  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="../Features.AssetAnalysis/Features.AssetAnalysis.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="../Features.AssetDelivery/Features.AssetDelivery.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="../Features.Tray/Features.Tray.csproj" />
    <!--
      The catalogue's table, its bookkeeping keys and its column list are declared in the contract
      assembly, which is the one place both the process that writes the catalogue and the process that
      reads it compile against. The scanner referenced nothing of the index until the catalogue became
      the indexing service's input rather than a document it parsed, and the reference exists so that a
      name cannot be changed on one side of that boundary and left behind on the other.
    -->
    <ProjectReference Include="../Scanner.IndexContract/Scanner.IndexContract.csproj" />
    <!--
      The references to Roblox.Wcf, Scanner.WcfServiceHost and ServiceInstallers
      were removed here. None of them was used by any source file in this
      project, and the two that target net48 could not be used anyway: a net8.0
      project cannot consume a .NET Framework assembly, so referencing one
      produced NU1702 on every build. That warning says the reference was
      resolved for a framework other than the one the project asks for, which
      means the link is unsound rather than merely untidy.
      The service supervision they provided is being replaced by RMS.ServiceHost
      and RMS.ServiceInstaller, which are .NET 8 and referenced where they are
      actually needed. Both legacy projects still build on their own; they are
      declared as their own steps in deploy/rmsdeploy.build.
    -->
  </ItemGroup>
</Project>
